rows=[(490, 'one-glass-before-carrying-on', 'One glass before carrying on', 'The capable professional returns to unequal domestic work and pauses for a glass of water. An interrupted ordinary kitchen task holds that exhaustion without inventing her face or glamorising self-sacrifice.', 'Original horizontal 3:2 matte gouache and graphite painting of a modest kitchen worktop at evening, a single plain clear tumbler half filled with water stands near the front left. Behind and right, one ordinary steel cooking pot with its lid resting partly over its rim; a loosely folded cotton wiping cloth lies partly cropped at the far edge. No meal styling or carefully scattered ingredients. Soft off-frame practical room light touches the water line, glass rim and a small part of the pot; back wall quiet grey cream. Glass has one coherent rim, straight sides, flat base and water surface ellipse; pot sits solidly with subdued reflections. Open space around the glass should feel like a short pause in unfinished work. No person, hands, office props, jewellery, lettering, perfect luxury kitchen, clutter or staged grime. Painted domestic realism with selective soft edges and matter-of-fact dignity.'), (492, 'where-water-no-longer-runs', 'Where water no longer runs', 'The poem’s inner desert is the evaporation of kindness and mercy. A shallow dry runnel through earth suggests that loss without drawing cacti in eyes, a burning heart or an arriving death carriage.', 'Original horizontal 3:2 austere charcoal and muted ochre watercolor, low oblique close landscape of a dry shallow water channel crossing a plain earth surface. A narrow irregular depression winds from lower left toward the middle distance, its fine silt lightly cracked in just a few places, banks low and naturally crumbled. Most of upper right is pale flat dusty wash with almost no detail. A few small embedded stones give scale; no epic dunes, cactus, dead tree, skull, animal, person, eye, sun disk or road. Diffuse hot daylight, gentle tonal contrast, no dramatic orange grading or decorative crack pattern repeated everywhere. The absence of moisture should feel ordinary and troubling, not a desert tourism poster. Sparse charcoal edge near the runnel; let other contours disappear.'), (494, 'after-the-harsh-word', 'After the harsh word', 'The opening compares a harsh note to a harmonium that is not ruined by one imperfect song; remorse follows words that hurt. A silent closed instrument after use allows that regret without literal injury, tears or an invented singer.', 'Original horizontal 3:2 restrained watercolor and warm brown pencil of a small ordinary wooden hand-pumped Indian harmonium resting on a plain low cotton floor mat after practice. Three-quarter view close enough to see a simple rectangular walnut-brown wooden case and compressed side bellows, with the plain protective keyboard lid CLOSED so no keys are visible. A small unadorned carrying handle on the near side; no ornate carving, brand label, decorative flowers or sacred symbols. The compressed bellows have a few believable nested folds connected to the case; instrument sits with weight on the mat, no floating panels. Soft morning light enters from one side, leaving the far wall and half the mat unresolved. Modest use wear at the handle only, no picturesque decay. Off-centre composition and quiet surrounding space, no musician, portrait, hands, notation, text or staged books. A pause after a difficult conversation, not a catalogue product image.')]
items=[]
